//! Batch 119 -- the ss16118/low-latency-nccl near-SoL collectives `kernel_opt`
//! distill (Plan 098 / Research 143).
//!
//! Source: `ss16118/low-latency-nccl` @ `5357eff325eddf978137de7140195a5568fa8a11`
//! (BSD-3-style NVIDIA license; shallow clone read + removed at landing) plus
//! the paper surface arXiv:2607.16100 "Every Microsecond Matters" (ETH Zurich +
//! NVIDIA). PROVENANCE corrections vs the verdict, verified at the pin: the
//! repo names the sentinel protocol POISON -- `ncclLLPoisonBuffer(Async)` fills
//! the scratch, `ncclLLBufferIsPoisoned` polls, and the receive path's Reset arm
//! re-poisons consumed slots (no `ncclLLBufferInitSentinel` exists); the host
//! sizing helper is `ncclCalcMinReductionBufferSize` (no
//! `ncclCalcScratchBufferSize`); and LL128 is ENUMERATED in the sync-mode enum
//! as "NOT YET IMPLEMENTED" with a bare `// TODO` in the receive path -- so the
//! LL128-atomic determinism caveat cites the PAPER's measured mechanism, never
//! shipped code. Honest caveats carried in-rule: the repo's poison table maps
//! by dtype SIZE, its "Floating-Point Type Overrides" section header is
//! followed by NO specializations, and a usage comment claims 0x7FC00000 /
//! 0xDEADBEEF where the table carries 0xFFFAFFFA-class NaN bit patterns -- the
//! int-dtype sentinel is a representable payload, a documented collision hazard.
//!
//! All throughput numbers are SOURCE-REPORTED (the paper's GB200 cells; barriers
//! at roughly 40% of a small-message `AllReduce`) -- never cite as ours; the
//! league and the workspace are single-GPU, so these rules serve USER code on
//! multi-GPU hardware we do not own (the B110 precedent). Rider (same batch,
//! existing rule): B58 output-buffer-rotation-pool-multi-forward gets the
//! bidirectional peer-exchange credit face. Batch 119 / Plan 098 / Research 143.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::corpus::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "sentinel-readiness-over-separate-flag".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "When device code must learn that a PEER kernel's writes have landed (cross-kernel / cross-rank producer-consumer through peer-accessible symmetric memory), a dtype SENTINEL protocol replaces the flag-word pair: initialize the peer-visible scratch ONCE with a sentinel payload (the source names it POISON), writers store DATA DIRECTLY (a plain full-width vector store -- no flag word, no separate signaling store, no data-to-flag ordering fence), readers poll each slot until it reads NON-sentinel ('okAll &= !is_poisoned(val)' over all slots), then consume. What it buys vs the flag pair: the flag ARRAY, every flag STORE, and one ordering fence are deleted, and the slot carries FULL payload width (16 data bytes per 16-byte slot vs 8+8) -- full effective bandwidth; the paper's own taxonomy makes this the BANDWIDTH-BOUND moderate-message arm of a three-way sync-mode decision (LL flag-in-data the latency-bound tiny arm). THE FOUR CONSTRAINTS: (a) NEVER-EQUAL-SENTINEL -- no valid payload may bit-match the sentinel; FP dtypes are safe only while the sentinel is a NaN bit pattern (the source's table: 1B 0x7F = fp8-NaN, 2B 0x7E00 = fp16/bf16-NaN, 4B 0xFFFAFFFA = negative-NaN bits, 8B 0xFFFAFFFAFFFAFFFA), but INTEGER dtypes have no NaN domain -- the source's 4-byte default IS a representable i32 (0xFFFAFFFA = -327686) that a legitimate payload could collide with, and its 'Floating-Point Type Overrides' section header is followed by NO specializations while a usage comment claims 0x7FC00000 / 0xDEADBEEF -- stale comments vs the table; pick the payload-foreign bit pattern per dtype EXPLICITLY and treat int-dtype sentinels as a probabilistic argument, never a structural one. (b) RESET-BEFORE-REUSE -- a consumed slot reads non-sentinel FOREVER after, so it must be re-poisoned before the next round (the source does this INSIDE the receive path: after the poll succeeds, the Reset arm writes the poison value back; standalone reset()/resetRange() exist too); without it the reader consumes STALE data as fresh -- the ABA hazard the LL epoch-flag protocol is structurally immune to. (c) PEER-STORE-VISIBILITY -- the protocol needs GPU-initiated peer access to symmetric memory so a writer's plain store becomes visible to the reader's polls; the fence-free claim is scoped to platforms where remote stores arrive coherent. (d) SINGLE-WRITER SLOTS -- one writer per slot means a slot transitions stale-data then fresh-data exactly once, so results are DETERMINISTIC (the property the LL128 atomic-ADD accumulation gives up -- see ll-flag-in-data-packed-poll's caveat). Bounded fix-space: pick a payload-foreign sentinel per dtype; poison at init AND re-poison consumed slots; poll all-slots-ready with a taken-branch hint; keep the slot map single-writer. Suggest-only fix class (a protocol restructure, not a mechanical edit). Regime: peer-written data polled by device readers, moderate bandwidth-bound messages. Does NOT fire for in-kernel masked-lane identity writes (that is mask-sentinel-matches-reduction-identity -- a sentinel for lanes WITHIN one kernel's reduction, not cross-kernel readiness). Distinct from stripe-sms-l2-semaphore-reduce (B92 -- the flag is stored AFTER the data behind an ld.global.acquire.gpu spin + fence.acq_rel: exactly the signaling step this protocol deletes), barrier-free-per-dependency-wait-from-loop-counters (host-side orchestration waits, not device peer polls), and ll-flag-in-data-packed-poll (same batch -- the tiny-message regime twin, epoch-band ABA defense instead of reset). Source: ss16118/low-latency-nccl @ 5357eff325eddf978137de7140195a5568fa8a11 (BSD-3 NVIDIA) -- src/include/nccl_device/ll_buffer.h:17-30 (the three sync modes + 'User MUST poison buffer before receiving'), :51-79 (ncclCalcMinReductionBufferSize / ncclLLGetPoisonValue / ncclLLPoisonBuffer(Async) -- the sentinel-init surface); src/nccl_device/ll_buffer.cu:70-76 (host sizing: LL doubles the buffer, the sentinel mode does NOT -- 'mode == ncclLL ? singleBufferSize * 2'), :52-66 (the host poison table maps by dtype SIZE), :85-92 (the fill kernel); src/include/nccl_device/impl/ll_buffer__types.h:79-110 (the poison trait table + the stale-comment mismatch), :206-215 (bit-reinterpret poison getter), :217-236 (IsPoisoned bit-level compare); src/include/nccl_device/impl/ll_buffer__funcs.h:759-777 (the all-slots non-poison poll), :779-791 (the Reset arm re-poisons in the receive path), :849-864 (reset()); test/unit/ll_buffer_test.cu:674-678 (poison-before-use at the API boundary), :338-361 (the bcast -> recvReduce Reset=true -> advanceEpoch consumer shape). Paper: arXiv:2607.16100 mechanism 2 'sentinel sync' + the measured basis (barriers ~= 40% of a small-message AllReduce; NCCL ring 11.0 -> their kernel 2.37 us on 4xGB200; SoL floor 1.404 us, their one-shot within 7%) -- absolute numbers on THEIR silicon, never ours. Batch 119 / Plan 098 / Research 143.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: a flag word per slot -- the writer stores data, THEN signals;\n// the reader spins on the flag and needs the data->flag order fenced.\nuse core::sync::atomic::Ordering;\nconst FLAG_READY: u32 = 1;\nstruct FlagSlot {\n    data: f32,\n    flag: u32,\n}\nfn peer_send(slot: &FlagSlot, v: f32) {\n    let d = &slot.data as *const f32 as *mut f32;\n    let f = &slot.flag as *const u32 as *mut u32;\n    unsafe {\n        core::ptr::write_volatile(d, v); // payload store\n    }\n    core::sync::atomic::fence(Ordering::Release); // the ordering tax\n    unsafe {\n        core::ptr::write_volatile(f, FLAG_READY); // + the signaling store\n    }\n}\nfn peer_recv(slot: &FlagSlot) -> f32 {\n    let f = &slot.flag as *const u32;\n    let d = &slot.data as *const f32;\n    loop {\n        if unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(f) } == FLAG_READY {\n            core::sync::atomic::fence(Ordering::Acquire);\n            return unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(d) };\n        }\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: sentinel readiness -- poison once, store FULL-WIDTH data\n// with no flag and no fence; the reader polls non-sentinel and RE-POISONS\n// on consume (reset-before-reuse is the ABA defense).\nconst POISON_BITS: u32 = 0xFFFA_FFFA; // negative-NaN payload: no finite f32 matches\nstruct SentinelSlot(f32); // ONE full-width word: no flag column at all\nfn poison_all(buf: &mut [SentinelSlot]) {\n    for s in buf.iter_mut() {\n        *s = SentinelSlot(f32::from_bits(POISON_BITS));\n    }\n}\nfn peer_send(slot: &SentinelSlot, v: f32) {\n    let p = slot as *const SentinelSlot as *mut f32;\n    unsafe {\n        core::ptr::write_volatile(p, v); // the value IS the signal\n    }\n}\nfn peer_recv_reset(slot: &mut SentinelSlot) -> f32 {\n    let p = slot as *const SentinelSlot as *const f32;\n    let val = loop {\n        let v = unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(p) }; // one load per poll\n        if v.to_bits() != POISON_BITS {\n            break v; // readiness == non-sentinel\n        }\n    };\n    *slot = SentinelSlot(f32::from_bits(POISON_BITS)); // reset BEFORE reuse\n    val\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "ll-flag-in-data-packed-poll".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "The tiny-message regime twin of sentinel readiness: pack the readiness flag INTO the data slot and ship both in ONE vector store -- {epoch_flag, data} per slot, width-matched (a 1-byte payload packs {flag, data} into one u16 store, 2-byte into u32, 4-byte into u64, 8-byte into a 128-bit {flag, flag, data0, data1} carrying TWO flag words, 16-byte splits across two slots). The writer's only cost is folding the epoch into the payload -- the separate signaling store and the data-to-flag ordering fence are gone because the pair moves as one atomic-width store; the reader unpacks {flag, val} from one vector load and polls flag == epoch ('okAll &= (flag == epoch)' then 'if (__builtin_expect(okAll, true)) break'). THE EPOCH BAND is the ABA defense: the counter starts at 2 and wraps 255 back to 2 ('epoch += (epoch == 255) ? 3 : 1' on a uint8_t -- 255 + 3 truncates to 2), so flags 0 and 1 are RESERVED (reset writes flag 0) and a slot's stale flag can never equal the live epoch WITHIN a wrap; ACROSS wraps epoch 2 recurs, which is exactly why LL mode REQUIRES multi-buffering (the constructor comment: 'Multiple buffering needs to be used to support LL or LL128 sync mode') -- the per-epoch sub-buffer rotation ('(subBuffer % roundRobinFactor) * pitch') makes the aliased old copy physically unreachable. THE COST is 2x slot width (halved effective bandwidth) + 2x scratch (the host sizing helper doubles for LL: 'mode == ncclLL ? singleBufferSize * 2') -- pay it where the payload is so small the signaling round-trip dominates (latency-bound tiny messages); the sentinel wins the bandwidth-bound moderate regime; the two-regime split by message size IS the decision. A multi-slot payload is ready only when every slot's flag matches: the 16-byte split enforces 'outFlag = (flag0 == flag1) ? flag0 : ~0u' -- mismatch encodes retry. DETERMINISM CAVEAT (the same paper's third mode, two-shot LL128-atomic): making the flag CARRY accumulation via atomic ADD (8 threads own one 128-byte line, element 0 displaced to smem and restored by 16 extra threads, contribution via atomic ADD into the target scratch, flag == N means all ranks contributed) is NON-DETERMINISTIC FP -- worst-case error bound gamma_{N-1} ~ 3.8e-6 at N=64 FP32, addition-only ops, dependent on NVLink cache-line atomicity (vectorized atomics f32/f16 only) -- FORBIDDEN for bit-identity or replay-gated consumers; the packed-poll pair keeps single-writer slots, so determinism is preserved. LL128 (120B data + 8B flag per line) is ENUMERATED in the source's mode enum as 'NOT YET IMPLEMENTED' with a bare TODO in the receive path -- cite the LL128-atomic mechanism to the PAPER's measurement, never to shipped code. Suggest-only fix class. Regime: latency-bound tiny payloads crossing peer-visible scratch. Distinct from stripe-sms-l2-semaphore-reduce (B92 -- the flag arrives AFTER the data in a separate store behind an acquire spin: the step this packs away), shared-counter-decode-producer-progress (ONE shared progress counter, not per-slot flags), barrier-free-per-dependency-wait-from-loop-counters (host-side orchestration), and sentinel-readiness-over-separate-flag (same batch -- the full-bandwidth moderate regime twin, reset-based ABA defense instead of the epoch band). Source: ss16118/low-latency-nccl @ 5357eff325eddf978137de7140195a5568fa8a11 (BSD-3 NVIDIA) -- src/include/nccl_device/ll_buffer.h:17-30 (ncclLL: '8 bytes data + 8 bytes flags per 16-byte slot'); src/include/nccl_device/impl/ll_buffer__funcs.h:242-340 (the packed stores: {epoch, data} one vector per width, the 64-bit {flag, flag, data0, data1} layout), :364-428 (the unpacked loads), :417-419 (the both-flags-match rule + the ~0u retry encoding), :730-740 (the flag == epoch poll + __builtin_expect), :476 (constructor epoch = 2), :881-885 (advanceEpoch + the 255-to-2 skip), :888-892 (resetEpoch), :480-488 (the multi-buffering requirement comment), :849-864 (LL reset stores {zero, flag 0}); src/nccl_device/ll_buffer.cu:70-76 (the 2x scratch); test/unit/ll_buffer_test.cu:356 (advanceEpoch per consumer iteration). Paper: arXiv:2607.16100 mechanism 1 (LL flag-in-data) + mechanism 4 (two-shot LL128-atomic: the determinism caveat and the gamma bound). Batch 119 / Plan 098 / Research 143.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: data and flag in SEPARATE stores -- every slot pays a signaling\n// store + a fence, and the reader pays two dependent loads per poll.\nuse core::sync::atomic::Ordering;\nstruct SplitSlot {\n    data: u32,\n    flag: u32,\n}\nfn post(slot: &SplitSlot, epoch: u32, v: u32) {\n    let d = &slot.data as *const u32 as *mut u32;\n    let f = &slot.flag as *const u32 as *mut u32;\n    unsafe {\n        core::ptr::write_volatile(d, v); // payload store\n    }\n    core::sync::atomic::fence(Ordering::Release);\n    unsafe {\n        core::ptr::write_volatile(f, epoch); // separate signaling store\n    }\n}\nfn wait(slot: &SplitSlot, epoch: u32) -> u32 {\n    let f = &slot.flag as *const u32;\n    let d = &slot.data as *const u32;\n    while unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(f) } != epoch {} // load 1\n    unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(d) } // load 2 (dependent)\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: {flag, data} packed into ONE u64 -- one store, one load,\n// no fence; epoch band 2..=255 with 0/1 reserved for the reset state.\n#[repr(C)]\nstruct PackedSlot(u64); // {flag: u32, data: u32} in one atomic-width word\nconst RESET_FLAG: u32 = 0; // reserved: never a live epoch\nfn pack(epoch: u32, v: u32) -> PackedSlot {\n    PackedSlot(((epoch as u64) << 32) | (v as u64)) // fold flag INTO the payload\n}\nfn post(slot: *mut PackedSlot, word: PackedSlot) {\n    unsafe {\n        core::ptr::write_volatile(slot, word); // flag + data ship together\n    }\n}\nfn wait(slot: *const PackedSlot, epoch: u32) -> u32 {\n    loop {\n        let w = unsafe { core::ptr::read_volatile(slot) }.0; // ONE load\n        if (w >> 32) as u32 == epoch {\n            return w as u32;\n        }\n    }\n}\nfn advance_epoch(epoch: u8) -> u8 {\n    // 255 + 3 truncates to 2: the counter cycles 2..=255, never 0 or 1.\n    epoch.wrapping_add(if epoch == 255 { 3 } else { 1 })\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
